  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 13-year-old girl, including fever, headache, photophobia, nausea, neck rigidity, and a maculopapular rash, suggests viral meningitis. The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis shows elevated white blood cell count with a predominance of lymphocytes (85%), which is characteristic of viral infections. The presence of a recent summer camp exposure raises the suspicion for common viral pathogens. Echovirus is a well-known cause of viral meningitis in children and is more prevalent in this context compared to Herpes simplex virus, which is less common in this age group. Bacterial meningitis is less likely due to the lymphocytic predominance in the CSF, and Listeria monocytogenes typically affects older adults. Therefore, the most likely causal organism is Echovirus.",
  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of the 13-year-old girl, including fever, headache, photophobia, nausea, neck rigidity, and a maculopapular rash, strongly suggests viral meningitis. The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis shows a white blood cell count of 280/mm3 with a predominance of lymphocytes (85%), which is characteristic of viral infections rather than bacterial. The clear appearance of the CSF and normal glucose levels further support a viral etiology. Given the recent history of attending summer camp, there is a higher likelihood of exposure to common viral pathogens, particularly enteroviruses such as Echovirus, which are known to cause viral meningitis in children. Other options, such as Herpes simplex virus, are less common in this age group, and bacterial causes like Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis are less likely due to the lymphocytic predominance in the CSF. Listeria monocytogenes typically affects older adults and immunocompromised individuals. Therefore, the most likely causal organism in this case is Echovirus, aligning with the clinical and laboratory findings.",
